  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   The central idea is to construct an improved step function h: [0, 2] \u2192 [0, 1] by using a deterministic spectral optimization method based on the symmetric group theory and harmonic analysis. Unlike random search methods, this approach leverages the inherent symmetry of the problem and uses a structured perturbation strategy to iteratively improve the solution. It differs from existing approaches by:\n   - Using analytical spectral methods instead of numerical optimization\n   - Incorporating both translational and rotational symmetry constraints\n   - Employing a hierarchical tuning of parameters\n\n   The solution space is restricted to functions with 2-fold rotational symmetry, which aligns with the fundamental periodicity of the overlap problem. For a function h(x) and its shifted version h(x+k), we require equivalence under translation. This symmetry reduction significantly narrows down the search space while guarding against suboptimal solutions.\n\n   The primary limitation is the potential for premature convergence to symmetric but suboptimal configurations. To mitigate this, we use a multi-start approach with different symmetry-breaking perturbations stored from the initial construction.\n\n2. Mathematical specifications:\n   We are optimizing the functional:\n     J(h) = max_{k} \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\n\n   subject to:\n     \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n     0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\n   Discrete representation with N points:\n     dx = 2/N\n     \u2211_{i=0}^{N-1} h[i] * dx = 1  \u21d2 \u2211_{i=0}^{N-1} h[i] = N/2\n\n   The discrete correlation is:\n     corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n     C5 = (max(corr)/N) * 2   (accounting for dx factor)\n\n   For spectral representation:\n     Let \u03c9 be the fundamental frequency: \u03c9 = 2\u03c0/N\n     Then, the Fourier transform of the constraint is:\n          \u03a6(k) = \u03a3 h[n] exp(-2\u03c0i k n/N)\n\n   3. Implementation procedure:\n\n     a. Initialization (using the provided parent):\n        - Downsample the parent if necessary based on current resolution\n        - Project to the symmetry subspace\n\n     b. Core update loop:\n        for iteration in range(max_iterations):\n            i. Take current h and create random perturbations:\n                - Symmetry-preserving\n                - Symmetry-breaking\n\n            ii. Compute correlation for all shifts\n            iii. Project to feasible set (clipping, scaling, symmetry enforcement)\n            iv. Adjust connectivity (pulse width/spacing)\n            v. Update to best candidate\n\n     c. Projection method:\n        Projection to feasible set: \n          h_feas = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n          total = np.sum(h_feas)\n          desired = N/2\n          scale = 1.0\n          scale = sum(h_feas) > 0, scale_strict = desired/sum(h_feas)\n          delta = scale_strict - scale\n          h_adjusted = h_feas * scale_strict\n          return np.clip(h_adjusted, 0, 1)\n\n     d. Stopping criteria:\n        - Time budget (100 seconds)\n        - Gradient stability measure\n        - Functional value threshold\n\n     e. Output: (h_values, c5_bound, N)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   Default parameters:\n     base_resolution = 320\n     symmetry_regularization = 0.8\n     connectivity_regularization = 0.6\n     perturbation_range = 0.4\n\n   Key variation points:\n     1. Pulsed structure:\n        Type A: Uniform pulse placement\n        Type B: Concentrated pulse distribution\n        Type C: Alternation between pulse widths\n\n     2. Symmetry enforcement:\n        - Strict 2-fold rotational symmetry\n        - Mean-shifted 2-fold symmetry\n        - Combined rotational and quarter-translational symmetry\n\n     3. Random selection weights:\n        - Primary: 0.7 (variation preservation)\n        - Secondary: 0.25 (pulse width)\n        - Tertiary: 0.05 (spatial shift)\n\n   5. Compute budget and robustness:\n     - Resolution test schedule: [320, 640, 1280] (i=0 to 2)\n     - Time per resolution: t_i = 40 * (N/max_base)^0.5 seconds\n     - The fourth resolution would be at N=2560, requiring 100 seconds\n     - Timeout management: if (time_left/(N/50) < 2) then skip higher resolutions\n\n   Initialization and fallback:\n     1. Always use the provided initial_h_values if available\n     2. For N larger than the parent's resolution, interpolate via:\n        h_new = np.interp(np.linspace(0,2,3*parent_size), \n                          np.linspace(0,2,parent_size), \n                          initial_h_values)\n     3. Integral adjustment: project_vector(h_initial, N)\n\n6. Other details:\n   Feasibility checks:\n     - Invariant checks: all values between 0 and 1 inclusive\n     - Integral preservation: |sum(h)*dx - 1| < 1e-2\n\n   Projection/Initialization helpers:\n   def project_vector(h, N):\n       h_clip = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n       total = np.sum(h_clip)\n       desired = N/2\n       scale = desired / total\n       h_scaled = h_clip * scale\n       return np.clip(h_scaled, 0, 1)",
